  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Appling colormaps to real data\n",
    "\n",
    "Now we'll apply some color maps to actual data in the wild!\n",
    "* data location for brain scan: http://use.yt/upload/a082bb26 (we probably won't get to this)\n",
    "* michigan depth map: https://www.ngdc.noaa.gov/mgg/greatlakes/michigan.html (binary float data linked on that page)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 85,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#import h5py # you may have to install this as well\n",
    "# might not get to the dataset were we use it \n",
    "#  however"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let's start with the Michigan dataset:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 86,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# depth in meters\n",
    "fn1 = \"/Users/jillnaiman/Downloads/michigan_lld.flt\"\n",
    "michigan = np.fromfile(fn1, dtype='f4').reshape((5365, 4201))\n",
    "# this reshape is just to go from a 1D array to the \n",
    "# correct 2d shape"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What does this data look like?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 87,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "(5365, 4201)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 87,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "michigan.shape"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let's take a quick look at a histogram of the michigan data:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 88,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.hist(michigan.flat)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "So, there looks to be a super weird data point:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 89,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "-9999.0"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 89,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "michigan.min()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Lets do an easy cleaning of this data for viz purposes. We'll set this odd point to an `NaN`:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 90,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "michigan[michigan == -9999] = np.nan"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can then use special min/max functions to re-calculate these quantities:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 91,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "(-275.89, 351.94348)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 91,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# what are new min & max\n",
    "np.nanmin(michigan), np.nanmax(michigan)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can also \"mask\" out this `NaN` value for plotting purposes:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 92,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# we can replot our histogram as well just to check it out\n",
    "plt.hist(michigan[~np.isnan(michigan)])\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "# here the mask is ~ = NOT an NaN"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We see that our data is nearly evenly distributed around 0 meters.  Measurements above zero are above the level of the lake, and negative numbers represent depths inside lake Michigan.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Goal: \n",
    "  1. to pick a colormap that shows both the large scale structure and interesting features of this dataset\n",
    "  1. to pick a colormap that demarkates where the lake and land are\n",
    "\n",
    "Now that we have a sense of our data, lets do a quick plot.  I'll ask matplotlib to also plot a colorbar so we can map colors to data-values:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 93,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.imshow(michigan)\n",
    "plt.colorbar()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Question: \n",
    "\n",
    "Now that we've had a chance to look at our data a bit, what do we think the values represent?  What does a positive value mean?  Negative value?  Where do we think, spatially, these things will lie?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Lets play around with this default colormap:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 94,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.imshow(michigan)\n",
    "plt.clim(0, 100) # only plot from depths of 0->100\n",
    "plt.colorbar(extend = 'both') # add little arrow ends\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Recall our min&max depth in meters:\n",
    "\n",
    " (-275.89, 351.94348)\n",
    " \n",
    "Let's make a symmetric color map about a min/max that encompasses our data min/max, using a *diverging* colormap:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 95,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "<matplotlib.colorbar.Colorbar at 0x7fdbf8286c50>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 95,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.imshow(michigan, cmap=\"seismic\")\n",
    "plt.clim(-352, 352) # lets remap over an evenly distributed color map\n",
    "plt.colorbar()\n",
    "# note this is an example of a divering colormap"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Ok, so we are close to doing what we want here, but boy is it hard to look at!  There are actually some colormaps that are built to highlight different parts of maps like this:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 96,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "<matplotlib.colorbar.Colorbar at 0x7fdbca220910>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 96,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.imshow(michigan, cmap=\"terrain\")\n",
    "plt.clim(-352, 352)\n",
    "plt.colorbar()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`terrain` is another diverging colormap, but here we see that the blue/green is probably water (negative height) and the land is highlighted with brown & white.  This follows our \"usual\" conventions of how we plot differences between land and water.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Now, lets say we want to show a sharp contrast in our color map from the negative to the positive numbers instead of the \"smeared\" out transition from land to water in the above map.  We can do this by rescaling the colormap with another matplotlib set of functions:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 97,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import matplotlib.colors as colors\n",
    "\n",
    "# if I haven't already, lets make a bigger pic\n",
    "plt.rcParams[\"figure.dpi\"] = 200 # or 100"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 98,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stderr",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "/Users/jillnaiman/anaconda3/envs/DataViz/lib/python3.7/site-packages/ipykernel_launcher.py:1: MatplotlibDeprecationWarning: default base may change from np.e to 10.  To suppress this warning specify the base keyword argument.\n",
      "  \"\"\"Entry point for launching an IPython kernel.\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1280x960 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.imshow(michigan, cmap=\"terrain\", norm = colors.SymLogNorm(10))\n",
    "plt.clim(-352, 352)\n",
    "plt.colorbar()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "If we now look at our image, we see some interesting things.  So, now there is a sharp contrast between negative & positive depths/heights and there is not as much contrast between blue/green or brown/white.\n",
    "\n",
    "But why?  Let's check out the docs for `SymLogNorm`:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 99,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "colors.SymLogNorm?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This is a symmetrical log scale so it logs things both in the negative & positive directions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 100,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "(array([0.     , 1.     , 1.69897]), array([0.     , 1.     , 1.69897]))"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 100,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "np.log10([1,10,50]),np.log10(np.abs([-1,-10,-50]))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We see that 1 and 10 are mapped to a jump of 1 but 1->50 is mapped only to a jump of 0.7 instead of 40."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The lake Michigan data is a very high resolution map, so we can zoom in to see some cool details:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 101,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stderr",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "/Users/jillnaiman/anaconda3/envs/DataViz/lib/python3.7/site-packages/ipykernel_launcher.py:1: MatplotlibDeprecationWarning: default base may change from np.e to 10.  To suppress this warning specify the base keyword argument.\n",
      "  \"\"\"Entry point for launching an IPython kernel.\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "(3300.0, 3900.0)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 101,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1280x960 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.imshow(michigan, cmap=\"terrain\", norm = colors.SymLogNorm(10))\n",
    "plt.clim(-352, 352)\n",
    "plt.colorbar()\n",
    "plt.xlim(2700, 3300)\n",
    "plt.ylim(3300, 3900)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This shows us one of the rivers that feed into lake Michigan."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "And just for fun, here is how it looks with our bad \"jet\" colormap:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 102,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stderr",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "/Users/jillnaiman/anaconda3/envs/DataViz/lib/python3.7/site-packages/ipykernel_launcher.py:1: MatplotlibDeprecationWarning: default base may change from np.e to 10.  To suppress this warning specify the base keyword argument.\n",
      "  \"\"\"Entry point for launching an IPython kernel.\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "<matplotlib.colorbar.Colorbar at 0x7fdbe8d71250>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 102,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1280x960 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.imshow(michigan, cmap=\"jet\", norm = colors.SymLogNorm(10))\n",
    "plt.clim(-352, 352)\n",
    "plt.colorbar()\n",
    "# ew."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can use our `ipywidgets` to build up a multiple-widget interface to check out this Michigan dataset that puts all of these things we've explored together:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 103,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json": {
       "model_id": "75735a6fd8894827b4329fe42a267337",
       "version_major": 2,
       "version_minor": 0
      },
      "text/plain": [
       "interactive(children=(Dropdown(description='colormap', index=154, options=('Accent', 'Accent_r', 'Blues', 'Blu…"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# if we haven't already\n",
    "import ipywidgets\n",
    "\n",
    "@ipywidgets.interact(colormap = plt.colormaps(),\n",
    "                     color_range = (1.0, 352.0, 1.0),\n",
    "                     sym_log=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "def plot(colormap = 'terrain', color_range = 352, sym_log = True):\n",
    "    if sym_log:\n",
    "        norm = colors.SymLogNorm(10)\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        norm = colors.Normalize()\n",
    "        \n",
    "    plt.imshow(michigan, cmap=colormap, norm = norm)\n",
    "    plt.clim(-color_range, color_range)\n",
    "    plt.colorbar(extend = 'both')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": []
  }
